  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Multisectoral Assistance to Vulnerable Populations in North Kivu (Coordination and Management of Displacement Sites and Non-food Items)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since March 2022, the Democratic Republic of the Congo has faced an acute humanitarian crisis due to the armed conflict between the armed forces and their allies and the March 23 Movement (M23). As of late 2022 to early 2023, the conflict has intensified, causing large-scale population displacement and acutely growing humanitarian needs. According to the Displacement Tracking Matrix, over 600,000 people have been displaced as a result of the M23 crisis as of July 2023. In response, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is providing emergency assistance to populations affected by the M23 crisis in North Kivu, in the form of shelter, non-food items, and camp coordination and camp management (CCCM) interventions. IOM is improving the living conditions of 5,630 households through the provision of essential household items. Under the scope of CCCM, IOM is strengthening the coordination and response of humanitarian actors providing humanitarian assistance and protection activities in displacement sites along the Kanyaruchinya axis, which is the location of many spontaneous collective centres without any support structures. Based on recommendations by the Gender-Based Violence sub-cluster, 21,000 solar lamps are also being distributed to people in these collective centres, as well as displacement sites in Goma and Nyiragongo.</narrative>
  </description>
